# Branch isolation

Work stays on `dreamforce-demo`, and nothing done here may reach the older
branches or the original `SMB-app` repo.

This checkout has a single remote, and it is the demo repo:

| Remote | Repo | Status |
|---|---|---|
| `origin` | `SMB-app-dreamforce-demo` | the only remote, and the valid push target |

`origin` is safe here **because of the URL it points at, not because of its
name.** In other checkouts of this project `origin` is the original `SMB-app`
and must never be pushed to. Verify the URL, never the remote name.

There are also no older branches in this checkout. `dreamforce-demo` is the
only branch, local or remote, so `smb-final-version`, `atlas-smb-app-v4`, and
`main` are not reachable until someone fetches them.

## Rules

- Never push to any remote whose URL is not `SMB-app-dreamforce-demo`. That
  includes `git push --all` and `git push --mirror`, which ignore the current
  branch and can fan out to every configured remote.
- Never commit to, rebase, amend, or force-push `smb-final-version`,
  `atlas-smb-app-v4`, `main`, or any other pre-existing branch, if one is ever
  fetched into this checkout.
- Never republish the GitHub Pages preview on `atlas-smb-app-v4`. The
  `preview/` folder may be edited on `dreamforce-demo` only.
- Branch off `dreamforce-demo`, not off the older branches.
- Do not delete or rename existing branches, local or remote.
- Rebasing your own unpushed commits on `dreamforce-demo` onto a newer
  `origin/dreamforce-demo` is fine; it rewrites only commits nobody else has.

## Before every push

Confirm the branch, its upstream, and the URL that upstream resolves to:

```bash
git branch --show-current               # expect: dreamforce-demo
git rev-parse --abbrev-ref @{upstream}  # expect: origin/dreamforce-demo
git remote get-url --push origin        # expect: .../SMB-app-dreamforce-demo.git
```

The third command is the one that matters. The first two can both look correct
while `origin` points somewhere it should not.

`push.default` is unset, so it defaults to `simple`, meaning a bare `git push`
from `dreamforce-demo` only ever reaches that branch's own upstream. Prefer a
bare `git push` over naming a remote and branch.

## Reading from older branches is fine

Inspecting history or copying a file out of an older branch does not modify it,
so this is allowed once such a branch has been fetched:

```bash
git show smb-final-version:path/to/file        # read
git checkout smb-final-version -- path/to/file # copy onto dreamforce-demo
```
